\section{Measurement Model}
\label{sec:measurement_model}

Accelerometer and magnetometer measurements provide corrections; the gyroscope
drives propagation.  A zero pseudo-measurement on the integral displacement
$\vct S$ provides regularization.

\subsection{Accelerometer model}
The body-frame specific-force measurement is
\begin{equation}
\vct{f}_b=\Rot_{wb}(\vct{a}_w-\vct{g}_w)+\vct{b}_a^{\mathrm{tot}}(T)+\vct{n}_a,
\label{eq:acc-meas}
\end{equation}
where $\vct{g}_w=[0,0,g]^{\top}$ in NED,
$\vct{n}_a\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mat{R}_a)$, and
\begin{equation}
\vct{b}_a^{\mathrm{tot}}(T)
=\vct{k}_a(T-T_{\mathrm{ref}})+\vct b_a.
\label{eq:acc-bias-temperature-model}
\end{equation}
Here $\vct b_a$ is the estimated slow OU residual about the calibrated
temperature-dependent mean, not the total physical bias.

For the left-multiplicative convention,
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial\vct{f}_b}{\partial\delta\vct{\theta}}
&=-\Skew{\hat{\vct{f}}_{\mathrm{cog},b}},&
\frac{\partial\vct{f}_b}{\partial\vct{a}_w}&=\Rot_{wb},&
\frac{\partial\vct{f}_b}{\partial\vct{b}_a}&=\Id.
\label{eq:acc-meas-jacobians}
\end{align}
where
$\hat{\vct{f}}_{\mathrm{cog},b}=\Rot_{wb}(\hat{\vct{a}}_w-\vct{g}_w)$.
The rank--3 correction therefore couples attitude, latent world acceleration,
and residual accelerometer bias.  OU rather than random-walk bias dynamics
change only prediction and process covariance, not this Jacobian.

\subsection{Magnetometer model}
With calibrated world reference $\vct{B}_w$,
\begin{equation}
\vct{m}_b=\Rot_{wb}\vct{B}_w+\vct{n}_m,
\qquad
\vct{n}_m\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mat{R}_m),
\label{eq:mag-meas}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\vct{m}_b}{\partial\delta\vct{\theta}}
=-\Skew{\hat{\vct{m}}_b}.
\label{eq:mag-meas-jacobian}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Integral pseudo-measurement}
The drift-control channel is
\begin{equation}
\vct{z}_S=0=\vct{S}+\vct{n}_S,
\qquad
\vct{n}_S\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mat{R}_S),
\label{eq:zero-pseudo}
\end{equation}
with
\begin{align}
\vct{r}_S
&:=\begin{bmatrix}r_{Sx}&r_{Sy}&r_{Sz}\end{bmatrix}^{\top},
\label{eq:rS-vector}\\
\mat{R}_S
&:=\diag\!\left(r_{Sx}^2,r_{Sy}^2,r_{Sz}^2\right),
\label{eq:RS-from-rS}\\
\mat{H}_S
&:=\begin{bmatrix}0&\cdots&\Id_S&\cdots&0\end{bmatrix}.
\label{eq:HS-selector}
\end{align}
Here $\vct r_S$ is the per-axis pseudo-measurement standard-deviation vector
(in $\mathrm{m\,s}$), and $\mat R_S$ is its covariance.

The pseudo-measurement is a regularizer rather than a physical observation.
Repeated updates create a soft leak: smaller $\vct r_S$ strengthens
low-frequency drift suppression, whereas larger values preserve slower motion
but allow more drift.

\paragraph{Time-based cadence}
Let $T_S>0$ be the configured pseudo-update period and $e_k$ the elapsed time
since the previous application.  At each variable-duration IMU step $h_k$,
\[
\tilde e_{k+1}=e_k+h_k.
\]
If $\tilde e_{k+1}<T_S$, no pseudo-update is performed and
$e_{k+1}=\tilde e_{k+1}$.  Otherwise the update is applied and
\begin{equation}
e_{k+1}=\tilde e_{k+1}\bmod T_S.
\label{eq:pseudo-elapsed-scheduler}
\end{equation}
A floating-point tolerance is used at the boundary.  Cadence is defined in
seconds, making the regularization rate independent of IMU sample rate.
Deadlines are served on the first subsequent IMU sample, so realized gaps are
grid-quantized and lie between one step and one configured period plus one
step.

\subsection{IMU lever-arm model}
If the IMU is displaced from the center of gravity by known body-frame vector
$\vct{r}_b$, the predicted specific force includes
\begin{equation}
\vct{f}_b=\vct{f}_{\mathrm{cog},b}
+\vct{\alpha}_b\times\vct{r}_b
+\vct{\omega}_b\times(\vct{\omega}_b\times\vct{r}_b)
+\vct{b}_a^{\mathrm{tot}}(T)+\vct{n}_a.
\label{eq:acc-meas-lever}
\end{equation}
The angular-rate and angular-acceleration terms are treated as known inputs.
Their second-order attitude-uncertainty coupling is neglected, so the state
Jacobians remain those of \eqref{eq:acc-meas-jacobians}; $\vct r_b=0$ disables
the correction.

The optional steady-heel transform is disabled in all reported results.
